[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Encapsulation in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Encapsulation is one of the fundamental principles of object-oriented programming (OOP). It describes the idea of bundling data (attributes) and methods that operate on that data within a single unit (a class or object), and restricting direct access to some of the object's components. This helps prevent accidental modification of data and makes the code more manageable and secure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Encapsulation"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Hiding: Protects the internal state of an object by preventing direct access from outside the object."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modularity: Makes code more modular and easier to understand, maintain, and debug."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Encapsulated objects can be easily reused in different parts of the application or in other projects."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Allows you to change the internal implementation of an object without affecting the code that uses it."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Encapsulation in JavaScript"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript, prior to ES6, did not have built-in support for access modifiers like 'private' or 'protected' found in other OOP languages. However, encapsulation can be achieved using conventions and closures."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Encapsulation with Closures (Pre-ES6)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Closures can be used to create private variables and methods within a function. These variables and methods are only accessible from within the function's scope, effectively hiding them from the outside world."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Example of encapsulation using closures
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // Private variable

  return {
    increment: function() {
      count++;
    },
    decrement: function() {
      count--;
    },
    getCount: function() {
      return count;
    }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
counter.increment();
counter.increment();
console.log(counter.getCount()); // Output: 2
// console.log(counter.count); // Error: counter.count is undefined"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'count' is a private variable because it's only accessible within the 'createCounter' function. The returned object provides methods ('increment', 'decrement', 'getCount') to interact with the private 'count' variable, but the variable itself cannot be directly accessed from outside."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Encapsulation with ES6 Classes"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"ES6 introduced classes, which provide a more structured way to implement OOP concepts. While ES6 classes don't have true private members by default, conventions and the use of symbols can help achieve a similar effect."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Conventions (Underscore Prefix)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common convention is to prefix private members with an underscore ('_'). This signals to other developers that these members should not be accessed or modified directly from outside the class. However, this is just a convention, and doesn't actually prevent access."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Example of encapsulation using ES6 classes and underscore convention
class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    this._name = name; // Convention for private member
    this._age = age;   // Convention for private member
  }

  getName() {
    return this._name;
  }

  getAge() {
    return this._age;
  }

  setAge(newAge) {
    if (newAge > 0) {
      this._age = newAge;
    } else {
      console.log("Age must be a positive number.");
    }
  }
}

const person = new Person("Alice", 30);
console.log(person.getName()); // Output: Alice
console.log(person._name); // Output: Alice (Still accessible, but discouraged)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, '_name' and '_age' are considered private members by convention. While they can still be accessed directly (as shown in the last line), it's understood that doing so is discouraged and could lead to unexpected behavior."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Symbols (More Robust)"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Symbols provide a more robust way to create private members in ES6 classes. Symbols are unique and immutable values that can be used as property keys. Because symbols are unique, it's difficult to access properties keyed by symbols from outside the class."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"// Example of encapsulation using ES6 classes and Symbols
const _name = Symbol('name');
const _age = Symbol('age');

class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    this[_name] = name;
    this[_age] = age;
  }

  getName() {
    return this[_name];
  }

  getAge() {
    return this[_age];
  }

  setAge(newAge) {
    if (newAge > 0) {
      this[_age] = newAge;
    } else {
      console.log("Age must be a positive number.");
    }
  }
}

const person = new Person("Bob", 25);
console.log(person.getName()); // Output: Bob
// console.log(person[_name]); // Error: Cannot access private property outside of class
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(person)); // Output: [Symbol(name), Symbol(age)]"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, '_name' and '_age' are Symbols.  While technically you can list the symbols used as properties on an object, you still cannot directly access the value without knowing the specific symbol. This provides a stronger form of encapsulation than the underscore convention."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Private Class Fields (ES2022+)"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"ES2022 introduced true private class fields, denoted by a '#' prefix. These fields are only accessible from within the class, providing the strongest level of encapsulation."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"// Example of encapsulation using ES2022 private class fields
class Person {
  #name;
  #age;

  constructor(name, age) {
    this.#name = name;
    this.#age = age;
  }

  getName() {
    return this.#name;
  }

  getAge() {
    return this.#age;
  }

  setAge(newAge) {
    if (newAge > 0) {
      this.#age = newAge;
    } else {
      console.log("Age must be a positive number.");
    }
  }
}

const person = new Person("Charlie", 40);
console.log(person.getName()); // Output: Charlie
// console.log(person.#name); // SyntaxError: Private field '#name' must be declared in an enclosing class"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, '#name' and '#age' are truly private class fields. Attempting to access them from outside the class will result in a SyntaxError."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Encapsulation is a crucial concept in OOP that helps create more maintainable, reusable, and secure code. JavaScript offers various ways to achieve encapsulation, ranging from closures and conventions to symbols and, most recently, true private class fields. Choosing the appropriate method depends on the specific requirements of your project and the level of encapsulation needed."}]
